    "## My Findings\n",
    "When comparing the mean values of those who only speak English and those who speak both English and Spanish. In the place of articulation the difference bewteen them was not what I was expecting. I thought that there would be a bigger difference, because the two languages differ In the sense that they have different overall structures and rules. I felt the Spanish would have a bigger influence, but they were relatively the same in this dataset.\n",
    "Below are the calculated differences\n",
    "\n",
    "          Differences\n",
    "\n",
    "1.  alveolar    2.016320\n",
    "\n",
    "2.  bilabial    4.792626\n",
    "\n",
    "3.  velar       1.726545\n",
    "\n",
    "Bilabial stood out the most, having the largest Difference of 4.79 ms. This can potentially suggest that at this specific Place of articulation, the differences can be seen the most.\n",
    "\n",
    "Future Directions\n",
    "\n",
    "When looking at the dataset, I noticed that although it does ask a participant if they know another language, it only does that. It doesn't determine just how proficient or fluent they are in that language. Inlcuding this could get an even deeper analysis of the overall differences or inlfuence another language can have.\n",
    "\n",
    "Another aspcect I believe has a big impact is how long the overall The Praat audio recording was. In the direction it says to speak at a \"normal speech rate\", but that can be vague because some people naturally speak faster/slower. In the dataset, there could be another column that has the total time of the audio and those can also be tokenized into group ranges like 15-25, 26-35, etc.\n"
